cards.body:
A linked list is a chain of nodes, each holding a value and a pointer to the next node; the head pointer points to the first node, and the last node's pointer is `NULL`.

```c
typedef struct Node {
    int value;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;
```

Each **node** is a separate struct, usually from `malloc`, so nodes can sit anywhere in memory. The **head pointer** is the one variable you keep: lose it and you lose the whole list. An empty list is simply `head == NULL`.

Why: Inserting or removing next to a node you already hold only rewires pointers. Nothing has to shift, unlike in an array.

Watch out: "Node" has a second meaning in circuits (a junction). Here it is one link of the chain. Reaching the $k$-th item means following $k$ pointers, which takes $O(k)$ time.

