What does this program print? It builds a **linked list** by inserting each new node at the head.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
typedef struct Node { int value; struct Node *next; } Node;
int main(void) {
    Node *head = NULL;
    for (int v = 1; v <= 3; v++) {
        Node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
        n->value = v; n->next = head; head = n;
    }
    for (Node *p = head; p; p = p->next) printf("%d ", p->value);
    while (head) { Node *t = head->next; free(head); head = t; }
    return 0;
}
```

This function should delete the first node holding `v` and return the new head. What is wrong with it, and how do you fix it?

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
typedef struct Node { int value; struct Node *next; } Node;
Node *remove_value(Node *head, int v) {
    Node *prev = NULL;
    for (Node *p = head; p; prev = p, p = p->next) {
        if (p->value != v) continue;
        free(p);
        if (prev) prev->next = p->next;
        else head = p->next;
        break;
    }
    return head;
}
```

cards.body:
It reads `p->next` after `free(p)`, a use-after-free. Unlink the node first (save `p->next`), then free it.

Fixed middle section:

```c
Node *next = p->next;
if (prev) prev->next = next;
else head = next;
free(p);
```

Why: Once `p` is freed, the memory may be reused, so `p->next` can hold anything. AddressSanitizer reports this as `heap-use-after-free`.

Key idea: Deleting has two cases. If the node has a predecessor (`prev`), that predecessor must skip over it. If it is the head, the head pointer itself must move. That is why the function returns the head: the caller has to store the result.

Watch out: A walk needs `prev` because a singly linked list can't step backwards. Forgetting the head case is the other classic bug.

